{
  "data": {
    "text": "Connectivity Patterns During Music Listening: Evidence for Action‑Based Processing in Musicians\nVinoo Alluri, Petri Toiviainen, Iballa Burunat, Marina Kliuchko, Peter Vuust, and Elvira Brattico\nHuman Brain Mapping 38:2955–2970, 2017. © 2017 Wiley Periodicals, Inc. DOI: 10.1002/hbm.23565\n\nParticipants\nThirty-six healthy participants with no history of neurological or psychological disorders participated in the fMRI experiment. The participant pool was equally divided between musically trained (n = 18) and untrained participants (n = 18). Both groups were comparable with respect to gender, age distribution, cognitive measures (Processing Speed and Working Memory Index Scores from the WAIS‑WMS III), and socioeconomic status (according to Hollingshead’s Four‑Factor Index). The musicians’ group was homogeneous in terms of the duration of their musical training and years of active instrument playing.\n\nStimuli and Instructions\nThree musical pieces were used in the experiment: Stream of Consciousness by Dream Theater; Adios Nonino by Astor Piazzolla; and Rite of Spring (Part I: Introduction, Augurs of Spring, Ritual of Abduction) by Igor Stravinsky. All pieces are instrumental and about 8 min long. The order of presentation was counter‑balanced across participants. Participants’ only task was to attentively listen to the music delivered via high‑quality MR‑compatible insert earphones while keeping their eyes open. At the end of each piece, participants orally rated liking, arousal, and familiarity on a discrete five‑point scale.\n\nEthics Approval\nThe study protocol proceeded on acceptance by the ethics committee of the Coordinating Board of the Helsinki and Uusimaa Hospital District.\n\nfMRI Data Acquisition and Preprocessing\nScanning was performed using a 3T MAGNETOM Skyra whole‑body scanner (Siemens Healthcare, Erlangen, Germany) and a standard 32‑channel head‑neck coil. Using a single‑shot gradient echo EPI sequence thirty‑three oblique slices (field of view = 192 × 192 mm; 64 × 64 matrix; slice thickness = 4 mm; interslice skip = 0 mm; echo time = 32 ms; flip angle = 75°; voxel size: 2 × 2 × 2 mm³) were acquired every 2 sec, providing whole‑brain coverage. T1‑weighted structural images (176 slices; field of view = 256 × 256 mm; matrix = 256 × 256; slice thickness = 1 mm; interslice skip = 0 mm; MPRAGE) were also collected.\nFunctional MRI scans were preprocessed in Matlab using SPM8, VBM5 for SPM, and customized scripts. For each participant, low‑resolution images were realigned on six dimensions using rigid‑body transformations (translation and rotation corrections did not exceed 2 mm and 2°), segmented into grey matter, white matter, and cerebrospinal fluid, and registered to the corresponding segmented high‑resolution T1‑weighted structural images. These were in turn normalized to the MNI segmented standard a priori tissue templates using a 12‑parameter affine transformation. Functional images were then spatially smoothed using an 8 mm FWHM Gaussian filter. Movement‑related variance components assessed by the six realignment parameters were regressed out from each voxel time series. Spline interpolation was used to detrend the fMRI data. Temporal filtering was performed by Gaussian smoothing (kernel width = 4 sec).\n\n\nTitle: Connectivity Patterns During Music Listening: Evidence for Action-Based Processing in Musicians\n\nConnectivity Analyses\nFor functional connectivity analysis, networks were generated by calculating, for each participant, the Pearson correlation between the time series of all possible combinations of voxel pairs, yielding a cross-correlation matrix representing the strength of association between each voxel pair.\n\nSubsequently, each correlation matrix was thresholded to generate a binary adjacency matrix. Fixed average degree thresholding was used, expressed as S = log(N)/log(K). In the present study, thresholds of S = 2.5 and S = 4.0 were used.\n\nStarting from the adjacency matrices, we calculated the node degree and global efficiency for each participant. To increase statistical power, data obtained with individual stimuli were concatenated before evaluating node degree.\n\nCommunity Structure Analysis\nCommunity structure analysis was performed using the QCut algorithm, which optimizes the modularity measure. Ten runs were performed for each participant, and the partition yielding the highest modularity value was retained. Scaled inclusivity was used to determine the consistency of community structure across participants."
  },
